# --- finney-memoirs: the 1876 Barnes/Oberlin scan, repaired against the others ---
#
# `memoirsofrevchar1876finn` is the base text (it keeps its end-of-line hyphens,
# so every wrapped word rejoins exactly), but its OCR is damaged throughout: v
# read as y ("conyerted"), R as E ("Eome", "Eev."), N as IST, G as Gr / G-, h as
# li, opening quotes as ^^, stray gutter marks, decorative drop caps ("'TTT'HEN"),
# printer's signature marks ("3*"), and the facsimile of a handwritten sermon
# outline read as text. Every repair was settled against the OTHER 1876 scans of
# the same printing - `memoirsofrevchar00finnuoft` for words (a word was replaced
# only where this scan reads a non-word and the witness a word, or by hand where
# both read words and the context decides: "hurst"/"burst", "Prom"/"From",
# "lie"/"he"), and all three (uoft + both Google scans) for punctuation (a mark
# changed only where at least two agree and none reads it as this scan does:
# "prayer and! conference", "converted there He was"). Blanked names, which the
# print sets as an initial and a long dash and the OCR mostly lost ("Squire W ,",
# "Judge Gr"), use one form throughout: "W——". The uoft scan also supplies pages
# 94-95, which this scan lost (the pair ending "cannot remember."). Nothing is
# reworded.
#
# Two lists, derived in ONE step from the raw scan to the stored text, so no pair
# rewrites another's output: book-wide single-word repairs first (every occurrence
# of the `old` token takes that repair), then contextual repairs, each `old`
# unique in the book. Proved by re-deriving the stored text from the raw scan for
# all 36 chapters (settled twice, identical).
